# Dates from engine/notation/chronicle.ts: penicillin 1928, the BBC's television service 1936,
# Gagarin 1961, Apollo 11 1969, the World Wide Web opened to all in 1991.
lesson history-a-hundred-years-in-tens v=1 format=teach grade=2 unit=2 subject=history levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "A hundred years in tens"
  goal "Count along a timeline in tens of years, name the ten years something happened in, and find how far apart two events were."

  look {
    scene 36x9 {
      timeline line from=1920 to=2000 step=10 years=[1928, 1936, 1961, 1969, 1991] labels=["Penicillin found", "First television service", "First person in space", "Moon landing", "World Wide Web"] at=canvas(0, 0)
    }
    say """
      A hundred years is a long time, so we count it in tens. The ten years from 1960 to 1969 are
      called the 1960s. In the 1920s a doctor found penicillin, a medicine made from mould. In the
      1930s the first television service began. In the 1960s the first person went into space and
      people walked on the Moon, and in the 1990s the World Wide Web opened to everyone.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.tens-between a=1920 b=1950
    }
    show history.tens-between a=1930 b=1960 easy-a=1940 easy-b=1960 hard-a=1920 hard-b=1980
    show history.which-decade k=0 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show history.century-empty-flag k=0 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show history.years-apart k=0 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show history.which-decade k=1 easy-k=1 hard-k=3
    show history.century-empty-flag k=1 easy-k=1 hard-k=3
    show history.years-apart k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=3
    level hard {
      show history.tens-between a=1940 b=2000
    }
  }
  story {
    level medium hard {
      show history.years-apart k=1 level=medium
    }
    level easy {
      show history.tens-between a=1930 b=1950
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.nearly-not-in k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.before-grandma-ten k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "The 1960s are the ten years from 1960 to 1969. Count a hundred years in ten jumps of ten."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that long stretches of time are measured in tens and hundreds of years, as a long
    distance is in kilometres. The way in counts jumps of ten along the line; the core names the
    ten years an event falls in, places an event from its neighbours and counts the years between
    two events; the story adds two more. The wrong idea to watch for is "nearly the next ten":
    1969 is still in the 1960s, which is the two-star question. If the child is stuck, count the
    tens aloud along the line, "nineteen twenty, nineteen thirty", touching each mark. Off the
    page, find the ten years each person in the family was born in. England's key stage 1 history
    asks for "events beyond living memory that are significant nationally or globally", and a
    timeline in tens is where a child first places them.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example counts from 1920 to 1950, the jumps are two to four tens, the events named are space and penicillin, and the years apart are counted between two close flags."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the jumps run to six and eight tens, the events to place are the Moon landing and the first television service, the years apart cross a ten, and in the three-star question Grandma turns ten after every event on the line."
  }
}
